Cloudberry's singlenode_isolation2 tests on the port: the differences from
Cloudberry's expected output that have been reviewed, and are the port's for
a reason of its own.

Each file is a test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl gives
it -- the lines removed and added, hunk by hunk, with no file names or line
numbers -- named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a pass
in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  run.sh counts a test as
passing when its difference is exactly the file's.  The differences below
are the same in both passes, but for bitmap_union's, lockmodes' and
runaway_query's.

bitmap_index_ao_sparse.diff
    One EXPLAIN's plan and nothing else: PostgreSQL 19's planner reads the
    column table of two million rows with a parallel scan, one worker under
    a Gather, where Cloudberry's single node, which plans no parallel scan
    unless asked (enable_parallel), has a Seq Scan.  The counts through the
    bitmap index over blocks far apart, which the test is for, are
    Cloudberry's.

bitmap_union.planner.diff, bitmap_union.orca.diff
    The EXPLAIN's plan; the count is Cloudberry's in both passes.  The test
    sets Cloudberry's fault simulate_bitmap_and, by which its planner is
    made to choose a BitmapAnd of the two bitmap indexes: PostgreSQL 19's
    planner, which no extension can make cost one, reads one index's bitmap
    and filters by the other column.  ORCA chooses the BitmapAnd by itself,
    and names the two conditions and the two scans in the other order.

lockmodes.planner.diff, lockmodes.orca.diff
    A locking clause, and nothing else: every lock of an INSERT, UPDATE,
    DELETE or plain SELECT is Cloudberry's.  For one, Cloudberry's single
    node takes what its coordinator takes without the global deadlock
    detector: the table's ExclusiveLock, as its parser opens it, and no row
    is locked -- its plans have no LockRows, and NOWAIT and SKIP LOCKED
    change nothing, the second session waiting for the table.  The port's
    one node is
    PostgreSQL there, whose rows can be locked and no segment's are to be:
    a heap table is held in RowShareLock and its rows are locked under a
    LockRows, so FOR UPDATE NOWAIT fails at once on a locked row and SKIP
    LOCKED returns the rest, where the test has both wait.  An
    append-optimized table, no row of which anyone can lock, is held in
    ExclusiveLock as Cloudberry holds it (gp_ao.c, gp_ao_query_lockmode());
    what differs there is incidental, the AccessShareLock under which
    Cloudberry's parser looks the table up first, and the plan's LockRows.
    The orca pass has one lock more: a DELETE of one partition holds
    AccessShareLock on the partitioned table too, which ORCA reads the
    partition's place in.

pg_views_concurrent_drop.diff
    A view dropped in one session while another reads its definition from
    pg_views: Greenplum made the definition come back NULL (MPP-25160,
    try_relation_open in ruleutils.c's make_viewdef), and PostgreSQL raises
    "could not open relation with OID ...", which the test's own comment
    says.  The port runs PostgreSQL 19's ruleutils.c, which no extension can
    change, so the second session gets PostgreSQL's error.  The OID is
    masked by ../init_file.

resource_queue.diff
    What pg_stat_activity and pg_locks say of a backend that waits for a
    resource queue's slot: its wait event, which gp_resource names
    ResourceQueue, is an extension's, of type Extension, where Cloudberry's
    has a type of its own; and pg_locks lists no queue, whose slots are
    held and waited for in gp_resource's shared memory, since PostgreSQL
    19's lock methods are fixed.  The waits themselves are Cloudberry's (see
    the isolation2 suite's resource_queue).

runaway_query.orca.diff
    Its EXPLAIN, with the fault set that fails ORCA: under ORCA, the port's
    second pass, the query fails with the fault's error, as Cloudberry's
    isolation2 output under ORCA has it (runaway_query_optimizer.out); this
    suite's expected output is Cloudberry's single node's, which plans with
    the planner, and shows its plan.

uao/max_concurrency_row and _column, uao/max_concurrency2_row and _column,
uao/modcount_vacuum_row and _column, uao/select_after_vacuum_row and _column,
uao/select_while_vacuum_serializable2_row and _column,
uao/vacuum_self_function_row and _column, uao/vacuum_self_serializable_row
and _column, uao/vacuum_self_serializable2_row and _column,
uao/vacuum_self_serializable3_row and _column
    The numbers of an append-optimized table's segment files, and in
    max_concurrency2 what follows from them.  Cloudberry keeps segment file
    0 for a session in utility mode, and its single node is one: the first
    rows of a table go to file 0, a VACUUM moves them to file 1, and a table
    may have 128 files, 0 to 127.  gp_ao has no such file -- a writer takes
    a file from 1, on one node as on a segment (ao_dml.c; the isolation2
    suite's uao/compaction_utility has the same difference for a segment's
    own session) -- so the rows are in file 1, a VACUUM moves them to file
    2, and a table has 127.  The counts of rows, the states and the
    modification counts beside each number are Cloudberry's, file for file.
    max_concurrency: the 127 sessions' files are 1 to 127, not 0 to 126.
    max_concurrency2: its 128th writer at once, which Cloudberry's single
    node gives file 127, is refused -- "could not find segment file to use
    for inserting into relation", all 127 files being written by other
    transactions -- as a 128th is refused on a cluster, in Cloudberry and
    here alike (the isolation2 suite's form of the test).
